      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Local anaesthetics provide relief from pain when applied locally to nerve tissue by blocking conduction of sensory nerve impulse from the receptor to the brain cortex."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This study aimed at evaluating local anaesthetic activity of the methanolic leaves extract of Lannea schimperi."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Six groups of five animals were used; groups I-IV were used for intracutaneous wheal test in guinea pigs for infiltration anaesthesia, while group V and VI were used for guinea pig corneal reflex method of surface anaesthesia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The result indicated a significant ( 0.05) dose dependent local anaesthetic activity of the methanolic leaves extract of Lannea schimperi with faster onset and longer duration of action at 24 mg/ml than at 12 mg/ml of the extract. Additions of 5 μg of adrenaline into the 24 mg/ml preparation also prolonged the duration of local anaesthetic activity of the extract. The extract at 24 mg/ml significantly (0.05) inhibited corneal reflex, lidocaine was used as a standard drug in positive control group, while normal saline was used as negative control in all the treated groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The patent data therefore revealed that the methanolic leaves extract Lannea schimperi possess local anaesthetic principles that may require further scientific elucidation."
        }
      ]},
